The 2011–12 season was the 118th season of competitive [[2011–12 in Scottish football|football]] by [[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]]. They finished top of the [[Scottish Premier League]] on 93 points.

==Background==
{{Main|2010–11 Celtic F.C. season}}

In the [[2010–11 Celtic F.C. season|2010–11 season]] Celtic finished second in the league having gained 92 points, one less than [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]]. They also lost to their [[Old Firm]] rivals in the [[2011 Scottish League Cup Final|2011 League Cup Final]], as Rangers won 2–1 after extra time. Celtic's only trophy came after they beat [[Motherwell F.C.|Motherwell]] 3–0 in the [[2011 Scottish Cup Final]]. Celtic were knocked out of both European competitions at the first stage.  [[2011 UEFA Europa League Final|Europa League final]] runners-up [[SC Braga|Braga]] knocked Celtic out of the [[2010–11 UEFA Champions League#Third qualifying round|Champions League third round]] after a 4–2 aggregate victory. [[FC Utrecht|Utrecht]] knocked Celtic out of the [[2010–11 UEFA Europa League#Play-off round|Europa League play-off round]] by the same score.

== Review ==

=== June ===
Several former [[Celtic F.C. Reserve and Youth squads|youth academy]] players left during the month of June. The most notable being [[Paul McGowan (footballer)|Paul McGowan]] who went to fellow [[Scottish Premier League]] side [[St Mirren F.C.|St Mirren]] after his contract expired.<ref name="McGowanStMirren">{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/13674444|title=St Mirren clinch deals for McGowan and Hasselbaink|publisher=BBC Sport|date=6 June 2011|access-date=7 June 2011}}</ref> [[Ryan Conroy]] was released by the club and joined [[Scottish First Division]] side [[Dundee F.C.|Dundee]].<ref name="ConroyDundee">{{cite news|url=http://www.thecourier.co.uk/Sport/Football/article/15039/new-signing-ryan-conroy-sets-sights-on-top-flight-with-dundee.html |title=New signing Ryan Conroy sets sights on top flight with Dundee |publisher=The Courier |date=21 June 2011 |access-date=21 June 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110817065103/http://www.thecourier.co.uk/Sport/Football/article/15039/new-signing-ryan-conroy-sets-sights-on-top-flight-with-dundee.html |archive-date=17 August 2011 }}</ref> [[Sean Fitzharris]] also went to the First Division as he joined [[Greenock Morton F.C.|Morton]]<ref name="FitzharrisMorton" />

=== July ===
On 1 July, Celtic confirmed the signings of [[Adam Matthews]] from [[Cardiff City F.C.|Cardiff City]],<ref name="MatthewsCardiff">{{cite news | url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/9407563.stm | title=Adam Matthews Joins Celtic | access-date=20 May 2011 | work=BBC Sport | date=25 February 2011}}</ref> [[Kelvin Wilson]] from [[Nottingham Forest F.C.|Nottingham Forest]]<ref name="WilsonForest">{{cite news | url=http://sport.scotsman.com/football/Kevin-Wilson-signs-precontract-to.6683856.jp | title=Kelvin Wilson signs pre contract | access-date=20 May 2011|work=Scotsman.com}}</ref> and [[Dylan McGeouch]] from [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]].<ref name="newkid">{{cite news |url=http://www.heraldscotland.com/sport/13040772.Celtic_5_St_Mirren_0__A_new_kid_in_town/ |title=Celtic 5 St Mirren 0: A new kid in town |work=The Herald |date=27 November 2011 |access-date=27 November 2011}}</ref> All three had already signed on [[Bosman ruling|pre-contract agreements]] and joined Celtic after their contracts expired. Three players also left the club after the expiry of their contracts. Former [[Germany national football team|Germany]] and [[Sweden men's national football team|Sweden]] internationals [[Andreas Hinkel]]<ref name="HinkelFreiburg"/> and [[Freddie Ljungberg]]<ref name="LjungbergShimizu"/> left along with [[Ben Hutchinson]].<ref name="HutchinsonKilmarnock"/>

Celtic's pre-season started off with a three-match tour of Australia.<ref>[http://sport.stv.tv/football/scottish-premier/celtic/250259-celtic-confirm-australian-pre-season-plans/ Celtic confirm Australian pre-season plans]{{Dead link|date=August 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }} STV Sport, 18 May 2011</ref> The first match was on 2 July and Celtic lost 1–0 against [[Central Coast Mariners FC|Central Coast Mariners]].<ref>{{cite web
 |url=http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=1219
 |title=Celtic Sunk by Mariners in Sydney
 |publisher=Celtic FC
 |date=2 July 2011
 |access-date=23 November 2011
 |url-status=dead
 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110808022156/http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=1219
 |archive-date=8 August 2011}}</ref>

On 7 July [[Niall McGinn]] joined English [[Football League One|League One]] side [[Brentford F.C.|Brentford]] on a season-long loan.<ref name="McGinnBrentford">{{cite news | url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/b/brentford/9533523.stm | title=Celtic winger Niall McGinn joins Brentford on loan | publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation | work=BBC Sport | date=8 July 2011 | access-date=27 July 2011}}</ref> The next day [[Graham Carey]] signed for [[St Mirren F.C.|St Mirren]] after Celtic agreed to cancel his contract.<ref name="CareyStMirren">{{cite news | url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/14080986 | title=St Mirren secure wing-back Graham Carey from Celtic | publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation | work=BBC Sport | date=8 July 2011 | access-date=27 July 2011| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110726201304/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/14080986.stm| archive-date= 26 July 2011 | url-status= live}}</ref>

On 9 July Celtic played the second match of their Australian tour and beat [[Perth Glory FC|Perth Glory]] 2–0.<ref>{{cite news
 |url=http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=1254
 |title=Glory for dominant Celtic in Perth
 |publisher=Celtic FC
 |date=9 July 2011
 |access-date=23 November 2011
 |url-status=dead
 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110814072745/http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=1254
 |archive-date=14 August 2011}}</ref> On the same day Celtic officially signed [[Victor Wanyama]] from [[Belgian Pro League]] club [[K. Beerschot AC]] on a four-year deal.<ref name="Wanyama">{{cite news
 |url          = http://www.eveningtimes.co.uk/sport/editor-s-picks/stipe-pletikosa-and-victor-wanyama-set-to-make-celtic-bow-1.1113107
 |title        = Stipe Pletikosa and Victor Wanyama set to make Celtic bow
 |publisher    = Evening Times
 |date         = 20 July 2011
 |access-date  = 23 November 2011
 |archive-date = 19 September 2011
 |archive-url  = https://web.archive.org/web/20110919193053/http://www.eveningtimes.co.uk/sport/editor-s-picks/stipe-pletikosa-and-victor-wanyama-set-to-make-celtic-bow-1.1113107
 |url-status   = dead
}}</ref><ref>{{cite web
 |url          = http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory.php?item=1253
 |archive-url  = https://archive.today/20130221083729/http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory.php?item=1253
 |url-status   = dead
 |archive-date = 21 February 2013
 |publisher    = Celtic FC
 |title        = Victor Wanyama is a Celt
 |date         = 9 July 2011
 |access-date   = 5 August 2011
}}</ref> He had signed for Celtic nine days earlier but had to wait to get a [[Work permit (United Kingdom)|work permit]].<ref>{{cite news
 |url         = http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory.php?item=1209
 |title       = Victor Wanyama joins Celtic subject to work permit
 |publisher   = Celtic FC
 |date        = 30 June 2011
 |access-date  = 23 November 2011
 |url-status  = dead
 |archive-url  = https://archive.today/20130221144205/http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory.php?item=1209
 |archive-date = 21 February 2013}}</ref>

On 13 July Celtic won the final match of their Australian tour as they beat [[Melbourne Victory FC|Melbourne Victory]] 1–0.<ref>{{cite news
 |url=http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=1266
 |title=Brown fires Hoops to victory in Melbourne
 |publisher=Celtic FC
 |date=13 July 2011
 |access-date=23 November 2011
 |url-status=dead
 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120405031257/http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=1266
 |archive-date=5 April 2012}}</ref>

Celtic's last friendly before the start of the league campaign was a 1–0 away win over [[Football League Championship|Championship]] side [[Cardiff City F.C.|Cardiff]].<ref>{{cite web
 |url=http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=1294
 |title=Stokes earns Celtic victory in Cardiff
 |publisher=Celtic FC
 |date=20 July 2011
 |access-date=5 August 2011
 |url-status=dead
 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120405031445/http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=1294
 |archive-date=5 April 2012}}</ref>

Due to the fact that the [[2011–12 Scottish Premier League]] started several weeks earlier than usual, Celtic's first competitive match came on 24 July. Goals from [[Ki Sung-yueng]] and [[Anthony Stokes]] secured victory over [[Hibernian F.C.|Hibernian]], although [[Gary Hooper]] also missed a penalty in the match played at [[Easter Road]].<ref>{{cite news
 | url        = https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/scot_prem/14242114.stm
 | title      = Hibernian 0–2 Celtic
 | publisher  = BBC Sport
 | date       = 24 July 2011
 | access-date = 23 November 2011}}</ref> Two days later [[Efraín Juárez]] moved to [[La Liga]] side [[Real Zaragoza]] on loan.<ref name="JuarezZaragoza">{{cite web | url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/14299003 | title=Celtic misfit Efrain Juarez joins Real Zaragoza on loan | publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation | work=BBC Sport | date=26 July 2011 | access-date=27 July 2011| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110727061425/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/14299003.stm| archive-date= 27 July 2011 | url-status= live}}</ref>

Celtic's next game was another friendly against [[FA Premier League|Premier League]] team [[Wolverhampton Wanderers F.C.|Wolverhampton Wanderers]] which they lost 2–0.<ref>{{cite news
 |url=http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=1328
 |title=Celtic beaten by Wolves in friendly
 |publisher=Celtic FC
 |date=27 July 2011
 |access-date=5 August 2011
 |url-status=dead
 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120405031504/http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=1328
 |archive-date=5 April 2012}}</ref>

Celtic then finished second in the [[2011 Dublin Super Cup]] after losing 2–0 to [[Inter Milan]] on 30 July and beating a [[League of Ireland XI]] 5–0, the next day. The other team competing was [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] who won the competition.<ref>[http://www.belfasttelegraph.co.uk/sport/football/scottish/birthday-boy-lennon-well-used-to-trouble-in-paradise-16016037.html Birthday boy Lennon well used to trouble in Paradise] Belfast Telegraph, 25 June 2011</ref>

=== August ===
On 3 August, Celtic lost 2–0 against English [[Premier League]] side [[Swansea City A.F.C.|Swansea City]] at the [[Liberty Stadium]].<ref>{{cite web
 |url=http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=1359
 |title=Swansea defeat closes Celtic's pre-season friendlies
 |publisher=Celtic FC
 |date=3 August 2011
 |access-date=5 August 2011
 |url-status=dead
 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120405031654/http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=1359
 |archive-date=5 April 2012}}</ref> The next day [[Darren O'Dea]] signed for English [[Football League Championship|Championship]] side [[Leeds United A.F.C.|Leeds United]] on a season long loan.<ref name="O'DeaLeeds">{{cite web |title=O'Dea seals Leeds loan |url=http://www.skysports.com/story/0,19528,12875_7080770,00.html |work=Sky Sports |publisher=BSkyB |date=4 August 2011 |access-date=4 August 2011 }}</ref> Celtic were then drawn against [[Swiss Cup]] winners [[FC Sion]] in the play-off round of the [[2011–12 Europa League|Europa League]].<ref>{{cite news
 |url          = http://sport.stv.tv/football/scottish-premier/celtic/265119-celtic-drawn-against-fc-sion-in-europa-league-play-off-round/
 |title        = Celtic drawn against FC Sion in Europa League play-off round
 |publisher    = STV
 |date         = 5 August 2011
 |access-date  = 5 August 2011
 |archive-date = 10 September 2011
 |archive-url  = https://web.archive.org/web/20110910073630/http://sport.stv.tv/football/scottish-premier/celtic/265119-celtic-drawn-against-fc-sion-in-europa-league-play-off-round/
 |url-status   = dead
}}</ref> On 7 August Celtic resumed their SPL campaign with a 1–0 win over [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]], [[Anthony Stokes]] scored the winner. However, the match was overshadowed by [[Emilio Izaguirre]] suffering a broken ankle which was expected to keep him out till the new year.<ref>{{cite news
 | url        = https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/aug/07/aberdeen-celtic-scottish-premier-league
 | title      = Emilio Izaguirre's broken leg clouds Celtic victory at Aberdeen
 | work       = guardian.co.uk
 | date       = 7 August 2011
 | access-date = 1 December 2011}}</ref> [[Josh Thompson (footballer)|Josh Thompson]] then went to Championship side [[Peterborough United F.C.|Peterborough]] on loan.<ref name="ThompsonPeterborough">{{cite web |title=Josh Thompson goes on loan to Peterborough |url=http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=1388 |work=celticfc.net |publisher=Celtic FC |date=9 August 2011 |access-date=9 August 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120405031724/http://www.celticfc.net/newsstory?item=1388 |archive-date=5 April 2012}}</ref>

On 17 August, [[Fraser Forster]] joined on a loan deal from [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]], having spent the previous season as first choice goalkeeper at Celtic.<ref name="ForsterNewcastle">{{cite news |title=Celtic return for goalkeeper Fraser Forster |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/14561780 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=17 August 2011 |access-date=17 August 2011 }}</ref>

On 21 August, Celtic suffered their first home defeat to [[St Johnstone F.C.|St Johnstone]] since 1998 when [[Dave Mackay (footballer born 1980)|Dave Mackay]] scored to give the away side a 1–0 victory.<ref>{{cite news
 | url        = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/14520317
 | title      = Celtic 0–1 St Johnstone
 | publisher  = BBC Sport
 | date       = 21 August 2011
 | access-date = 28 September 2011}}</ref>

[[Richie Towell]] went to [[Hibernian F.C.|Hibs]] for a second loan spell.<ref name="TowellHibernian">{{cite news |title=Celtic's Richie Towell rejoins Hibernian
|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/14669133 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=25 August 2011 |access-date=25 August 2011 }}</ref> [[Daryl Murphy]] went on loan to [[Ipswich Town F.C.|Ipswich Town]].<ref name="MurphyIpswich">{{cite news |title=Murphy makes Town move|url=http://www.skysports.com/story/0,19528,12875_7123548,00.html |work=Sky Sports |publisher=BSkyB |date=25 August 2011 |access-date=25 August 2011 }}</ref>

[[Greig Spence]] went on loan to [[Scottish First Division|First Division]] club [[Hamilton Academical F.C.|Hamilton]].<ref name="SpenceHamilton">{{cite web|title=Spence Signs |url=http://www.acciesfc.co.uk/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=1566:spence-signs&catid=44:news&Itemid=140 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120615061436/http://www.acciesfc.co.uk/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=1566%3Aspence-signs&catid=44%3Anews&Itemid=140 |url-status=dead |archive-date=15 June 2012 |work=AcciesFC.co.uk |publisher=Hamilton Academical FC |date=26 August 2011 |access-date=27 August 2011 }}</ref>

[[Mohamed Bangura (footballer)|Mohamed Bangura]] also joined the club in a £2.2 million deal from [[AIK Stockholm]].<ref name="BanguraAIK">{{cite web |title=Bhoys tie up Bangura deal |url=http://www.skysports.com/story/0,19528,12875_7138801,00.html |work=Sky Sports |publisher=BSkyB |date=30 August 2011 |access-date=30 August 2011 }}</ref>

[[Badr El Kaddouri]] joined on a six-month loan from [[FC Dynamo Kyiv|Dynamo Kyiv]].<ref name="ElKaddouriDynamoKiev">{{cite news |title=Dynamo Kiev left-back El Kaddouri joins Celtic
|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/14740011 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=31 August 2011 |access-date=31 August 2011 }}</ref>
[[Jos Hooiveld]] went to [[Southampton F.C.|Southampton]] on a six-month loan.<ref name="HooiveldSouthampton">{{cite news |title=Hooiveld switches to Saints |url=http://www.skysports.com/story/0,19528,12875_7141581,00.html |work=Sky Sports |publisher=BSkyB |date=1 September 2011 |access-date=1 September 2011 }}</ref> [[Shaun Maloney]] moved to English [[Premier League]] side [[Wigan Athletic F.C.|Wigan Athletic]] for £1 million.<ref name="MaloneyWigan">{{cite news |title=Wigan complete £1m capture of Celtic's Shaun Maloney |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/14717548 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=31 August 2011 |access-date=31 August 2011 }}</ref>

=== September ===
Sion won 3–1 on aggregate, however were found guilty of fielding ineligible players by [[UEFA]] and were excluded from the competition. As a result of this Celtic were awarded both matches as 3–0 wins<ref name="SionOut">{{cite news
 | url        = https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/sep/02/celtic-europa-league-fc-sion
 | title      = Celtic back in Europa League after successful protest against FC Sion
 | publisher  = Guardian News and Media
 | work       = guardian.co.uk
 | date       = 2 September 2011
 | access-date = 21 September 2011}}</ref> and took Sion's place in the group stage along with [[Atlético Madrid]], [[Udinese Calcio|Udinese]] and [[Stade Rennais F.C.|Stade Rennais]].<ref>{{cite news
 | url        = http://uk.eurosport.yahoo.com/02092011/63/celtic-reinstated-europa-league.html
 | title      = Celtic reinstated to Europa League
 | publisher  = Yahoo/Eurosport
 | date       = 2 September 2011
 | access-date = 2 September 2011}}{{dead link|date=May 2015}}</ref>

On 5 September, [[Morten Rasmussen (footballer born January 1985)|Morten Rasmussen]] joined Turkish [[Süper Lig]] side [[Sivasspor]], on a short-term loan, until December.<ref name="RasmussenSivasspor">{{cite news |title=Sivasspor Celtic's Morten Rasmussen joins Sivasspor on loan|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/14808485 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=5 September 2011 |access-date=23 November 2011 }}</ref>

Celtic drew [[Ross County F.C.|Ross County]] in the third round of the [[2011–12 Scottish League Cup|League Cup]]<ref>{{cite news
 | url        = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/14708089
 | title      = Celtic draw Ross County in League Cup third round
 | publisher  = BBC Sport
 | date       = 29 August 2011
 | access-date = 12 September 2011}}</ref> and beat the home side 2–0 at [[Victoria Park, Dingwall|Victoria Park]].<ref>{{cite news
 | url        = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/14910253
 | title      = Ross County 0–2 Celtic
 | publisher  = BBC Sport
 | date       = 21 September 2011
 | access-date = 21 September 2011}}</ref> Celtic were then drawn to play Hibs in the quarter-final.<ref>{{cite news
 | url        = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/15022519
 | title      = Scottish League Cup draw sees Hibernian host Celtic
 | publisher  = BBC Sport
 | date       = 22 September 2011
 | access-date = 28 September 2011}}</ref>

===October===
On 2 October, Celtic lost 2–0 to [[Heart of Midlothian F.C.|Hearts]] at [[Tynecastle Stadium|Tynecastle]] with winger [[Kris Commons]] getting sent off. This defeat left Celtic in third place, 10 points behind leaders Rangers.<ref>[https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/15051297 Hearts 2-0 Celtic] BBC Sport 2 October 2011 |accessdate=30 December 2011</ref> In the following match, Celtic drew 3–3 with [[Kilmarnock F.C.|Kilmarnock]] at [[Rugby Park]]. Kilmarnock had been leading 3–0 at half-time but two goals from [[Anthony Stokes]] and a [[Charlie Mulgrew]] header brought Celtic back into it.<ref>{{cite news| title = Kilmarnock 3-3 Celtic | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/15226287 | publisher = BBC Sport|date= 15 October 2011|access-date=30 December 2011}}</ref> Following the Kilmarnock game, Celtic travelled to France to play [[Stade Rennais F.C.|Rennes]] in [[UEFA Europa League]] action. Rennes led 1–0 at half-time after a mix-up between [[Cha Du-ri]] and [[Fraser Forster]], but [[Joe Ledley]] scored in the second half with a header to secure a draw.<ref>{{cite news| title = Rennes 1-1 Celtic | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/15285228 | publisher = BBC Sport|date= 20 October 2011|access-date=15 January 2012}}</ref> The Bhoys returned to [[Scottish Premier League]] action defeating [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]] 2–1 at [[Celtic Park]] on 23 October 2011.<ref>{{cite news| title = Celtic 2-1 Aberdeen | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/15325494 | publisher = BBC Sport|date= 23 October 2011|access-date=15 January 2012}}</ref> [[Hibernian F.C.|Hibernian]] were the opponents for the final two games of October. Celtic won 4–1 in the [[Scottish League Cup]]<ref>{{cite news| title = Hibernian 1-4 Celtic | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/15363366 | publisher = BBC Sport|date= 26 October 2011|access-date=15 January 2012}}</ref> before the Edinburgh team secured a 0–0 draw in the [[Scottish Premier League]] tie at Celtic Park on 29 October 2011.<ref>{{cite news| title = Celtic 0-0 Hibernian | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/15413677 | publisher = BBC Sport|date= 29 October 2011|access-date=15 January 2012}}</ref>

=== November ===
[[Stade Rennais F.C.|Stade Rennais]] were Celtic's first opponents of November in the Europa League. Celtic won the match 3-1 thanks to an [[Anthony Stokes]] double and a third from [[Gary Hooper]].<ref>{{cite news| title = Celtic 3-1 Rennes | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/15472980 | publisher = BBC Sport|date= 3 November 2011|access-date=15 January 2012}}</ref> On 5 November 2011, Celtic were third in the [[Scottish Premier League]] behind leaders [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]], who were 15 points clear of their rivals, and [[Motherwell F.C.|Motherwell]].<ref>{{cite news| title = Rangers 3-1 Dundee Utd | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/15504383 | publisher = BBC Sport|date= 5 November 2011|access-date=15 January 2012}}</ref> Celtic then won four Scottish Premier League ties in November, defeating [[Motherwell F.C.|Motherwell]] 2–1,<ref>{{cite news| title = Motherwell1-2 Celtic | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/15510763 | publisher = BBC Sport|date= 6 November 2011|access-date=15 January 2012}}</ref> [[Inverness Caledonian Thistle F.C.|Inverness Caledonian Thistle]] 2–0,<ref>{{cite news| title = Inverness CT 0-2 Celtic  | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/15704917 | publisher = BBC Sport|date= 19 November 2011|access-date=15 January 2012}}</ref> [[Dunfermline Athletic F.C.|Dunfermline]] 2-1<ref>{{cite news| title = Celtic 2-1 Dunfermline | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/15751367 | publisher = BBC Sport|date= 23 November 2011|access-date=15 January 2012}}</ref> and [[St Mirren F.C.|St Mirren]] 5–0.<ref>{{cite news| title = Celtic 5-0 St Mirren | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/15802741 | publisher = BBC Sport|date= 26 November 2011|access-date=15 January 2012}}</ref> November ended poorly for Celtic after [[Atlético Madrid]] won 1–0 in a [[UEFA Europa League]] tie at Celtic Park, thanks to a goal from [[Arda Turan]] in the second half.<ref>{{cite news| title = Celtic 0-1 Atlético Madrid | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/15850210 | publisher = BBC Sport|date= 30 November 2011|access-date=15 January 2012}}</ref> However, the excellent league form during November ensured Celtic won a clean-sweep of monthly awards with [[Neil Lennon]] winning SPL Manager of the Month, [[Gary Hooper]] winning SPL Player of the Month and [[James Forrest (footballer, born 1991)|James Forrest]] winning SPL Young Player of the Month.<ref name="November Awards">{{cite news| title = Celtic's Neil Lennon, Gary Hooper and James Forrest win awards | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/16110735 | publisher = BBC Sport|date= 9 December 2011|access-date=15 January 2012}}</ref>

=== December ===
Celtic continued their excellent league form throughout December, winning all five of their [[Scottish Premier League]] games. A solitary goal from [[Gary Hooper]] in the first half was enough to defeat [[Dundee United F.C.|Dundee Utd]]<ref>{{cite news| title = Dundee Utd 0-1 Celtic | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/15908095 | publisher = BBC Sport|date= 4 December 2011|access-date=15 January 2012}}</ref> whilst a [[Victor Wanyama]] strike from 25 yards saw off the challenge of [[Heart of Midlothian F.C.|Hearts]] at Celtic Park the following week.<ref>{{cite news| title = Celtic 1-0 Hearts | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/16015615 | publisher = BBC Sport|date= 10 December 2011|access-date=15 January 2012}}</ref> Celtic travelled to Italy to face [[Udinese Calcio|Udinese]] in the [[UEFA Europa League]] and claimed a 1–1 draw, a result which meant they would exit the tournament at the group stages.<ref>{{cite news| title = Udinese 1-1 Celtic | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/16081808 | publisher = BBC Sport|date= 15 December 2011|access-date=15 January 2012}}</ref> [[Gary Hooper]] added to his seasonal goal tally in a 2–0 win over [[St Johnstone F.C.|St Johnstone]] at [[McDiarmid Park]].<ref>{{cite news| title = St Johnstone 0-2 Celtic | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/16129462 | publisher = BBC Sport|date= 18 December 2011|access-date=15 January 2012}}</ref> [[Georgios Samaras]] scored both in a 2–1 home win versus [[Kilmarnock F.C.|Kilmarnock]].<ref>{{cite news| title = Celtic 2-1 Kilmarnock | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/16229733 | publisher = BBC Sport|date= 24 December 2011|access-date=15 January 2012}}</ref> Celtic's last league game of 2011 was a home game against arch-rivals [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]]. [[Joe Ledley]] scored the only goal of the game as Celtic won 1–0.<ref>{{cite news| title = Celtic 1-0 Rangers | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/16281414 | publisher = BBC Sport|date= 28 December 2011|access-date=15 January 2012}}</ref> The result meant that Celtic had overturned a 15-point deficit in the [[Scottish Premier League]] and moved top of the league, two points clear of Rangers in the title race.

